---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const TB = "server/tests/test_browser.py";

const H = {
  short: "What every page is checked for, and in which browsers",
  how: "Real browsers against a real server",
  rules: "Accessibility, the policy, presses and traces",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      Browser tests run in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit, against the real server with its
      production settings and content security policy. Every page they visit is checked with axe
      for WCAG 2.2 AA in light and dark, for policy violations and for page errors, and a fixture
      fails any test where a press and its release land on different targets (<Adr n={[29, 102]} />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <p>
      The tests drive Playwright against the server as it's deployed: production settings, the
      collected static files and uvicorn, with Trusted Types enforced (<Path
        p={TB}
        sym='BROWSERS = ["chromium", "firefox", "webkit"]'
      />). They need the built web app (<code>make web</code>) and the browsers the locked
      Playwright asks for (<code>make test-browsers</code>); without a browser, pytest fails and
      says how to install it. A test is a browser test when it uses the browser or Playwright
      fixtures, or is marked <code>browser</code> or <code>built_web_app</code>, and CI runs those
      in the three browser shards (<PageLink to="Gates and CI" hash="rules" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Every page passes axe and breaks no rule" open>
        <p>
          A test calls <code>assert_clean</code> on each page it visits: no policy violations, no
          page errors, and nothing axe finds against WCAG 2.2 AA. It waits for fades to finish first,
          since text mid-fade has less contrast than it ends with (<Path p={TB} sym="def assert_clean(" />).
          The production policy is the one in force, so a page that would break it in production
          fails here (<PageLink to="Serving and security policy" />).
        </p>
        <p>
          On a few pages Playwright's Firefox crashes its content process when axe runs, so those
          pass <code>axe_in_firefox=False</code> and leave axe to the other two engines; the DOM axe
          checks is the same in each. Helpers find elements by role rather than by label, since
          Firefox also crashes on labels after axe.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Presses that land where they began">
        <p>
          An autouse fixture installs a script in every page and frame of a browser test's contexts.
          It records each press and release and every layout shift, and the test fails when a press
          and its release land on different targets (<Path
            p="server/tests/conftest.py"
            sym="def _watch_browser_click_stability("
          />, <Path p="server/tests/click_stability.py" sym="def finish(" />). <code
            >IKI_CLICK_STABILITY=report</code
          > records without failing; CI keeps each shard's reports for a week. Why pages hold still,
          and how, is on <PageLink to="Pages that hold still" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Nothing gets out of a page">
        <p>
          The exfiltration tests run in all three browsers: code in a plugin's frame tries every way
          out, and values written to attack Iki's own pages must render only as text (<Path
            p="server/tests/test_browser_exfiltration.py"
          />). <PageLink to="Plugin frames" hash="rules" /> has what each refusal rests on.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Failing traces are kept">
        <p>
          When a browser test fails, CI keeps its context traces for three days as an artifact of
          the shard; a local gate leaves them under <code>build/browser-failures/</code>, and the test
          VM's wrapper fetches them back (<Path p="scripts/ci/with-browser-traces.sh" />, <Path
            p="CONTRIBUTING.md"
          />). Traces come from disposable test contexts, but they hold what the test saw and sent.
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={TB} />: the fixtures and the app's own browser tests</li>
        <li><Path p="server/tests/click_stability.js" />: the press recorder</li>
        <li><Path p="server/tests/test_oligo_components.py" />: a plugin's components, tested in their frames</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0029" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0102" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
